  Last question before I present the design. The skill I'm following asks this once, up front, because tooling is
  cheapest to add before any code exists.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what gets set up now versus retrofitted later onto code already written to no standard.

  - [1mUnit tests ([38;5;153mnode:test[39m)[0m — zero dependencies, [38;5;153mnpm test[39m runs it. I'd write the prefs module test-first regardless; this
    just makes the tests runnable by you and by CI.
  - [1mLint + format (Biome or ESLint+Prettier)[0m — one dev dependency, catches the class of bug that a two-file app won't
    otherwise notice. Adds the repo's first [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m.
  - [1mEnd-to-end (Playwright)[0m — would actually exercise [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m in a real browser across a real reload, which is the
    behavior we care about. But it's a heavy dependency and a browser download for one assertion.

  Cheap to add later: lint (mechanical, just fix what it flags). Less cheap: tests, because retrofitting them tends to
  mean reshaping the code to be testable.

  [1mMy recommendation: unit tests only.[0m Dependency-free, covers the storage logic including the corrupt-data and
  quota-exceeded paths, and I'll verify the browser round-trip by hand rather than pulling in Playwright for a two-file
  app.

